WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
An episode records an event; semantic memory consolidates a preference.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
Either can persist. Storage does not determine what the record means.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.867
A checkpoint can persist too, but its contract is resuming execution, not learning a preference.

4
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:29.217
In episode e17, the user requests follow-up by email.

5
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:36.067
e24 and e31 add observations. Each event retains its time, subject and provenance.

6
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:43.417
The sequence describes specific experiences; it is not yet a universal rule.

7
00:00:43.917 --> 00:00:50.767
Three episodes support a preference for email.

8
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:57.617
Consolidation creates a reusable record linked to those three episodes.

9
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:04.967
A later correction can supersede it. Keeping only the conclusion loses its basis.

10
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:12.317
Memory says the account had a Pro plan yesterday.

11
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:19.167
The billing source says paid access is revoked today.

12
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:26.833
Current authorisation governs the operation. Relevant memory cannot replace the system of record.

13
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:34.183
The model proposes saving an observation. The proposal is not durable memory yet.

14
00:01:34.183 --> 00:01:41.033
The application checks subject, sensitivity, duplication and evidence before writing.

15
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:48.383
A proposal from another tenant is rejected; a valid observation keeps its provenance.

16
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:55.733
The email preference feeds both a memory record and a user summary.

17
00:01:55.733 --> 00:02:02.583
The user changes it to phone calls. The event invalidates dependent representations.

18
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:09.933
Updating only the original leaves stale copies. Lineage makes those copies discoverable.

